        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "heading_tag": "h1",
        "max_width": 46,
        "eyebrow": "How to make masah",
        "heading": "How to make masah on socks",
        "body": "<p>Masah means wiping over your socks during wudu instead of washing your feet. This page explains what it is, the conditions scholars set, how it’s done and how long it lasts.</p>",
        "footnote": "<p>We make socks. We’re not scholars. Everything here is drawn from published sources, which are named at the bottom of the page, and none of it is a ruling. For a ruling that applies to you, ask a scholar you trust.</p>"
      }
    },
    "conditions": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "anchor_id": "conditions",
        "eyebrow": "The conditions",
        "heading": "What the socks have to do",
        "lede": "<p>Different scholars set slightly different conditions, and some treat them as practical guidance rather than strict requirements. These are the ones that come up again and again.</p>",
        "footnote": "Al-Salam Institute records the last three as conditions “some jurists propose”, and describes them as practical rather than affecting the core ruling. Other scholars treat them as necessary. That difference is real and long-standing, and it isn’t one a sock company can settle."
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Put on after a full wudu",
            "body": "<p>The socks go on after a complete wudu in which the feet were washed. This one isn’t disputed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Covering the ankle",
            "body": "<p>The covering has to come over the ankle. Both khuff and cloth socks do, which is much of why many scholars applied the same ruling to both.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Strong enough to walk in",
            "body": "<p>The classical wording is that the footwear should stand up to walking a distance without tearing — usually put at around three miles.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Water shouldn’t easily soak through",
            "body": "<p>Stated by many scholars, and the condition a waterproof sock speaks to most directly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i5": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Staying on without being tied",
            "body": "<p>The covering should stay on the foot in normal use rather than needing constant adjustment.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
