    "words": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "anchor_id": "two-words",
        "eyebrow": "Two words",
        "heading": "Khuff and jawrab aren’t the same thing",
        "lede": "<p>Most of the disagreement about masah on socks comes back to this, and it’s rarely explained simply.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Khuff — leather footwear",
            "body": "<p>Khuff, plural khuffain, means leather footwear that covers the ankle. Closer to a boot than a sock. Wiping over khuffain is recorded in the authentic hadith collections and is accepted across all four Sunni schools.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Jawrab — cloth socks",
            "body": "<p>Jawrab is the word for socks made of cloth rather than leather. Dr Akram Nadwi of the Al-Salam Institute in Oxford notes it came into Arabic from the Persian gor-e-pa, meaning “grave of the foot” — the Arabs had no word of their own for something they rarely wore.</p>",
            "link_label": "alsalam.ac.uk/wiping-over-socks",
            "link_url": "https://alsalam.ac.uk/wiping-over-socks"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where the discussion sits",
            "body": "<p>The question was never really whether you can wipe over khuffain. It’s whether cloth socks share enough of the same properties to take the same ruling, and if so, which properties matter.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3"
      ]
    },
    "method": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "steps",
        "numbered": true,
        "anchor_id": "method",
        "eyebrow": "The method",
        "heading": "How masah is done",
        "lede": "<p>Described as it’s commonly taught. The details of hand position differ between the schools, and those differences are set out further down.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do a full wudu first, then put the socks on",
            "body": "<p>The feet are washed as part of a complete wudu before the socks go on. Masah applies from the next wudu onwards, not this one.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "At your next wudu, wipe instead of washing",
            "body": "<p>Go through wudu as normal. When you reach the feet, wipe over the socks rather than washing the feet.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Wet hands, over the top of the foot",
            "body": "<p>With wet hands, wipe over the upper surface of each sock, moving from the toes towards the shin. Right hand for the right foot, left for the left.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Top surface only",
            "body": "<p>The wiping is of the upper surface. The sole isn’t wiped.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3",
        "i4"
      ]
    },
    "duration": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "anchor_id": "how-long",
        "eyebrow": "How long",
        "heading": "How long masah lasts",
        "lede": "<p>This is the part most commonly misunderstood, and it’s misunderstood in one particular way.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "At home: twenty-four hours",
            "body": "<p>Someone who isn’t travelling in the sense the Sharia recognises may wipe for one day and one night.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Travelling: seventy-two hours",
            "body": "<p>Someone who is a traveller in that sense may wipe for three days and three nights.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The clock starts at the first wipe",
            "body": "<p>This is the bit people get wrong. The period runs from the first masah you make after your wudu breaks — not from when you put the socks on. Sources including IslamQA state this directly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When the time is up",
            "body": "<p>Once the period ends, masah is no longer valid. The socks come off and the feet are washed for the next wudu.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
